  "findings": [
    "At 600/s, declines cut the scorer's CPU from a median 2.64 to 2.10 ms per authorization and the stack's from 8.34 to 7.69 ms (all three declines runs below all three always runs on both measures). p99 fell from a median 461 to 348 ms and every declines run completed 9,001 or 8,986 of 9,001.",
    "57% of authorizations in this workload score at or above 0.1, so declines still runs TreeSHAP for most requests; the saving is about the 43% that are approved by the model.",
    "At 750/s, close to saturation, the two pairs showed no difference (scorer 2.67 and 2.90 ms for always, 2.62 and 2.63 for declines; total 8.10 and 8.63 against 8.56 and 8.87). Contention noise there is larger than the saving.",
    "One always run at 750/s completed all 11,250 scheduled authorizations, which the earlier single 750/s run did not; the knee near 730/s is approximate."
  ],
  "decision": "compose.yaml defaults PAYGUARD_EXPLAIN to declines. Scores, decisions and case openings are unchanged, and every case still has reason codes (fraud/tests/test_score_explain.py). Authorizations the model approves now carry an empty modelReasonCodes list. The library default stays always.",
  "limits": [
    "one machine with k6 on it",
    "three runs per setting at 600/s, two at 750/s",
    "synthetic workload and model; the saving depends on the share of declines"
  ]
